\section{Default mathematics}{
With \code{normalize = "none"} (the default), exposure for node \code{i} is
\deqn{E_i = \sum_j W_{ij} X_j}
the raw weighted sum over alters \code{j}. With \code{normalize = "row"}, this is
divided by \eqn{\sum_j W_{ij}}, giving average rather than total exposure.
These are different substantive quantities and are never interchanged
silently -- see the Direction and Isolates sections below.
}

\section{Direction}{
There is no single universally correct way to turn a \verb{(source, target)} tie
into the exposure convention \eqn{W_{ij}} = "the strength with which \code{j}
influences \code{i}" -- it depends on what the tie means. \code{direction} makes this
explicit:
\itemize{
\item \code{"source_influences_target"} (default): a row with \verb{source = A, target = B} means A influences B. Appropriate for ties like "sends
information to" or "informs".
\item \code{"target_influences_source"}: the same row means B influences A.
Appropriate for ties like "seeks advice from" or "receives
communication from", where the tie's target is the one exerting
influence.
}
Picking the wrong one silently reverses who is exposed to whom, so check
this against how your tie variable was actually defined.
}

\section{Incoming, outgoing, and both}{
\code{direction} declares what a stored row \emph{means}, and that settles which
neighbors contribute:\tabular{ll}{
   Intent \tab How to say it \cr
   exposure from \strong{incoming} ties \tab \code{direction = "source_influences_target"} \cr
   exposure from \strong{outgoing} ties \tab \code{direction = "target_influences_source"} \cr
   exposure from ties in \strong{either} direction \tab \verb{via = symmetrize(<network>, rule = )} \cr
}


"Both" is a \strong{derived network}, not a third \code{direction} value. Symmetrizing
is a substantive choice -- "tied if either direction exists" and "tied only
if both do" are different graphs, and mean/sum/max of the two weights are
different weighted graphs again -- so it is made explicitly, with the rule
recorded, rather than hidden inside a flag that otherwise only declares a
reading of the data.
}

