\section{Isolates}{
A node with no reference-set members (no relevant ties) has undefined
average exposure under row normalization, since the denominator is zero.
\code{isolate_rule} controls what such a node gets: \code{"na"} (default, \code{NA}),
\code{"zero"}, or \code{"error"} (refuse to compute at all while isolates are
present). Under \code{normalize = "none"}, isolates always get \code{0} with no
ambiguity, since an empty sum is \code{0}. A row whose weights are partly missing,
or whose signed weights cancel to zero, is not an isolate: its denominator is
undefined and its exposure is \code{NA} whatever \code{isolate_rule} says.
}

\section{An unobserved network is not an empty one}{
An isolate is a node observed to have no ties. That is a different thing
from a network nobody recorded, and the two must not get the same answer:
exposure \code{0} for every node at a wave that was never measured would
attenuate an estimated exposure effect toward zero with nothing to show for
it.

When the conduit is \strong{dynamic} (its values vary by wave, so ties are
wave-indexed), a group with an observed network at some waves and no record of
one at the requested wave is treated as unobserved there: exposure is \code{NA} for
every node in that group, and one warning names every affected group-wave
snapshot. \code{NA} rather than an error, because the data is incomplete rather
than malformed -- \code{\link[=fit_influence]{fit_influence()}} then drops those rows and reports them
like any other missingness.

A wave \strong{recorded as observed} with no ties is an empty network, not an
unobserved one: every node there is an isolate, so exposure is \code{0} under
\code{normalize = "none"} and follows \code{isolate_rule} otherwise, with no warning.
An all-zero matrix in \code{\link[=netis_data_from_adjacency_series]{netis_data_from_adjacency_series()}} and an edgeless
graph in \code{\link[=netis_data_from_igraph_list]{netis_data_from_igraph_list()}} are recorded this way, and for a tie
table \code{netis_data(observed_tie_waves = )} declares it. See Empty and unobserved
waves on \code{\link[=netis_data]{netis_data()}}.

This is deliberately narrow. A \strong{static} conduit is reused across waves
and so has no per-wave existence to be missing. A group with no observed network
at \emph{any} wave has not established that its network exists at all, so absence
cannot be inferred there. An isolate inside an observed network still gets
\code{0}. None of those cases produces \code{NA}.

A path-dependent mechanism such as \code{\link[=bi]{bi()}} reads the \emph{prior} waves, so a focal
wave with no ties of its own still has a well-defined exposure. The same test
applies to those prior waves instead: one with no ties, in a group with ties at
other waves, makes the exposure \code{NA} for every node in that group, and the
warning names that wave.
}

\section{Self-ties}{
A self-tie is an ordinary tie to this function, so a node's own value would
count as exposure to an alter. Under \code{self_ties = "prohibited"}, the default in
\code{\link[=netis_data]{netis_data()}}, a self-tie row with a nonzero value is refused. Under
\code{self_ties = "permitted"} it enters the sum, and the node is exposed to its own
value. \code{\link[=bi]{bi()}} refuses a self-loop in either case, because \code{gamma} already
supplies that quantity.
}

\section{Custom kernels}{
\code{mechanism} may be a \verb{function(context, spec)}, called once per group.
\code{context$W} is the group's weight matrix, oriented so that \code{W[i, j]} is the
strength with which \code{j} influences \code{i}, with any normalization already applied;
\code{context$X} is the content vector in the same node order. Either may hold \code{NA}.
The function returns a numeric vector of \code{length(context$X)}, or a matrix with
that many rows and named columns, one output column each.

Two attributes on the function declare what it needs:
\code{attr(kernel, "requires")} is \code{"focal"} (the default), or
\code{c("focal", "w_history", "x_history")} to also receive \code{context$W_history} and
\code{context$X_history}, lists holding the waves before the requested one, most
recent first. Nothing else is accepted. \code{attr(kernel, "supports_normalize")} is
\code{"row"} to allow \code{normalize = "row"}, which is otherwise refused.
}

